ag.py  |    |  \ndiag.submit  | diag.submit  |    |  \ndiag_run_cluster.sh  | diag_run_cluster.sh  |    |  \nenvironment.yaml  | environment.yaml  |    |  \neval_vis_decoder.py  | eval_vis_decoder.py  |    |  \neval_vis_decoder.submit  | eval_vis_decoder.submit  |    |  \ngpu_test.sh  | gpu_test.sh  |    |  \nplan.py  | plan.py  |    |  \nplan.submit  | plan.submit  |    |  \nplanning_run_cluster.sh  | planning_run_cluster.sh  |    |  \nplanning_run_local.sh  | planning_run_local.sh  |    |  \nplot_linear_geometry.sh  | plot_linear_geometry.sh  |    |  \nplot_pca.sh  | plot_pca.sh  |    |  \nplot_probe.sh  | plot_probe.sh  |    |  \nplot_temporal_distance.sh  | plot_temporal_distance.sh  |    |  \nplot_umap.sh  | plot_umap.sh  |    |  \nprecompute.submit  | precompute.submit  |    |  \nprecompute_dino_embs.py  | precompute_dino_embs.py  |    |  \npreprocessor.py  | preprocessor.py  |    |  \npyproject.toml  | pyproject.toml  |    |  \nsearch_spaces.py  | search_spaces.py  |    |  \nsetup_remote_host.sh  | setup_remote_host.sh  |    |  \nstart_remote_debugging.sh  | start_remote_debugging.sh  |    |  \nsubmit_eval_vis_decoder_job.sh  | submit_eval_vis_decoder_job.sh  |    |  \nsubmit_job.sh  | submit_job.sh  |    |  \nsubmit_sweep.sh  | submit_sweep.sh  |    |  \nsubmit_vis_decoder_job.sh  | submit_vis_decoder_job.sh  |    |  \ntest.env  | test.env  |    |  \ntrain.py  | train.py  |    |  \ntrain.submit  | train.submit  |    |  \ntrain_sweep.py  | train_sweep.py  |    |  \ntrain_vis_decoder.py  | train_vis_decoder.py  |    |  \ntrain_vis_decoder.submit  | train_vis_decoder.submit  |    |  \nutils.py  | utils.py  |    |  \n\n# Extended README — Codebase Overview\n\nThis repository is a fork of DINO-WM (Zhou et al., 2024, arXiv:2411.04983). This appendix summarises the structure of the codebase and, in particular, draws a clear line between the functionality inherited from the original DINO-WM release and the extensions we developed for this thesis.\n\nUnless a component is explicitly listed under Our Contributions below, it is part of the original DINO-WM repository and should be attributed to the original authors.\n\n* * *\n\n## 1. Inherited from DINO-WM (original authors)\n\nThe following form the unmodified backbone of the project and are not our work:\n\n  * The core world-model formulation: a frozen DINOv2 patch encoder, a ViT-based predictor over patch tokens, an action/proprio encoder, and an optional pixel decoder (models/visual_world_model.py, models/vit.py, models/vqvae.py).\n  * The zero-shot planning pipeline (CEM / gradient-descent MPC) and its evaluators (plan.py, planning/).\n  * The simulated environments and their datasets — PushT, PointMaze, Wall, and the deformable suite (env/, datasets/).\n  * The Hydra-based training entry point and configuration scaffolding (train.py, conf/).\n\nThe original project documentation is preserved in DINOWM_README.md.\n\n* * *\n\n## 2. Our Contributions\n\nOur central research direction is to insert a trainable, JEPA-style latent encoder on top of the frozen DINOv2 features and to study, through a battery of regularization losses and diagnostics, what structure that latent space must have to support reliable planning. All items in this section are our own work.\n\n### 2.1 JEPA latent world model\n\n  * A lightweight JEPA projection encoder that compresses DINOv2 patch embeddings into a learned latent space (models/jepa_encoder.py).\n  * An online / EMA-target training scheme (BYOL/JEPA-style): the online encoder is trained, while a target encoder is updated as an exponential moving average and provides the prediction targets (`update_target_network`, online/target wiring in models/visual_world_model.py and train.py).\n  * Integration of the JEPA encoder into checkpointing, resume, and the predictor loss path, plus extensive latent-collapse logging (online/target std, norms, and cosine-similarity diagnostics between predicted, online, target, and raw DINO embeddings).\n\n### 2.2 Self-supervised regularization losses\n\nAll of the following loss terms were added by us to shape the JEPA latent space (implemented in models/visual_world_model.py, configured per run under conf/runtype/):\n\n  * VICReg variance/covariance regularization to prevent latent collapse.\n  * Structure-preservation loss — keeps the pairwise patch-similarity structure of DINO intact through the projection.\n  * Temporal-distance loss — a margin loss enforcing that Euclidean latent distance grows with temporal separation of frames (requires a negative-frame sample emitted by the slicer, also added by us).\n  * Local smoothness loss — penalises latent change that is large relative to the action that caused it.\n  * Perturbation smoothness loss — penalises latent sensitivity to small random action perturbations.\n\nThese are toggled and weighted through dedicated runtype configs we introduced: baseline, jepa_base, jepa_smoothness, jepa_temporal, and jepa_smooth_perturb.\n\n### 2.3 Diagnostic and probing suite\n\nA set of standalone analysis tools we wrote to characterise the geometry of a trained latent space (scripts/, diag.py):\n\n  * Linear probes: pose, orientation, inverse dynamics, and a time-leakage falsification probe (`probe_pose.py`, `probe_orientation.py`, `probe_inverse_dynamics.py`, `probe_time_leak.py`).\n  * Geometry probes: global distance–distance regression and a local k-NN metric-preservation ratio (`probe_geometry.py`, `probe_knn_local.py`).\n  * Latent visualisations: PCA, UMAP, and temporal-distance plots (`plot_latent_pca.py`, `plot_latent_umap.py`, `plot_temporal_distance.py`), with convenience launchers (`plot_*.sh`).\n  * Training-time diagnostics (diag.py): action sensitivity, smoothness ratio, perturbation ratio, and multi-step rollout error.\n\n### 2.4 Visualization decoder\n\nA separate decoder, trained on a frozen JEPA world model, that reconstructs pixels from latents to make the learned representation inspectable (models/decoder/jepa_vis_decoder.py, train_vis_decoder.py, eval_vis_decoder.py). The evaluator produces `[input_t | decode(z_t) | decode(z_pred) | input_{t+1}]` comparison grids and latent-collapse metrics.\n\n### 2.5 Training infrastructure and efficiency\n\nNote: We thank Pascal Sager for providing the initial SLURM integration and base training optimizations, upon which we built the extended infrastructure and improvements detailed below.\n\nEngineering work we added to run the above at scale on a SLURM cluster:\n\n  * SLURM integration with automatic requeue, checkpoint resume, and collision-safe job handling (train.submit, submit_job.sh, and the various `*.submit` / `*_run_cluster.sh` scripts).\n  * Multi-GPU training and early stopping with state persisted across requeues.\n  * Throughput improvements: `torch.compile`, bf16 mixed precision, GPU-side image transforms (datasets/img_transforms_gpu.py), memory-mapped datasets (datasets/pusht_dset_efficient.py, convert_pusht_to_memmap.py), and an offline DINOv2 embedding precompute step (precompute_dino_embs.py).\n  * Compute presets for local CPU/GPU/MPS and cluster execution (conf/compute/), plus a remote-debugging setup (start_remote_debugging.sh).\n  * Planning extensions: additional planning-quality metrics (elite-action variance, latent-shift vs. action magnitude, feature utilisation, step-size diagnostics) and latent-space planning options (planning/, plan.py).\n\n### 2.6 Hyperparameter sweeping\n\nAn Optuna-based sweep over the JEPA loss configuration, with a conditional search space (dependent weights are only sampled when their parent toggle is on) and each trial dispatched as its own SLURM job (conf/sweep.yaml, search_spaces.py, train_sweep.py, submit_sweep.sh).\n\n* * *\n\n## 3. Command reference\n\nDay-to-day commands for training, planning, the visualization decoder, and cluster usage are collected in WABU_README.md.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNavForesee: A Unified Vision-Language World Model for Hierarchical Planning and Dual-Horizon Navigation Prediction (https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.01550)\nciteturn41academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Title: NavForesee: A Unified Vision-Language World Model for Hierarchical Planning and Dual-Horizon Navigation Prediction\nTitle: NavForesee: A Unified Vision-Language World Model for Hierarchical Planning and Dual-Horizon Navigation Prediction\nAuthors: Fei Liu, Shichao Xie, Minghua Luo, Zedong Chu, Junjun Hu, Xiaolong Wu, Mu Xu\nDate: Mon Dec  1 11:24:16 2025\n\nEmbodied navigation for long-horizon tasks, guided by complex natural language instructions, remains a formidable challenge in artificial intelligence. Existing agents often struggle with robust long-term planning about unseen environments, leading to high failure rates. To address these limitations, we introduce NavForesee, a novel Vision-Language Model (VLM) that unifies high-level language planning and predictive world model imagination within a single, unified framework. Our approach empowers a single VLM to concurrently perform planning and predictive foresight. Conditioned on the full instruction and historical observations, the model is trained to understand the navigation instructions by decomposing the task, tracking its progress, and formulating the subsequent sub-goal. Simultaneously, it functions as a generative world model, providing crucial foresight by predicting short-term environmental dynamics and long-term navigation milestones. The VLM's structured plan guides its targeted prediction, while the imagined future provides rich context to inform the navigation actions, creating a powerful internal feedback loop of perception-planning/prediction-action. We demonstrate through extensive experiments on the R2R-CE and RxR-CE benchmark that NavForesee achieves highly competitive performance in complex scenarios. Our work highlights the immense potential of fusing explicit language planning with implicit spatiotemporal prediction, paving the way for more intelligent and capable embodied agents.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCalibrated Value-Aware Model Learning with Stochastic Environment Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.22772)\nciteturn41academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Title: Calibrated Value-Aware Model Learning with Stochastic Environment Models ... The idea of value-aware model learning, that models should produce accurate value estimates, has gained prominence in model-based reinforcement learning.\nTitle: Calibrated Value-Aware Model Learning with Stochastic Environment Models\nAuthors: Claas Voelcker, Anastasiia Pedan, Arash Ahmadian, Romina Abachi, Igor Gilitschenski, Amir-massoud Farahmand\nDate: Wed May 28 18:40:49 2025\n\nThe idea of value-aware model learning, that models should produce accurate value estimates, has gained prominence in model-based reinforcement learning. The MuZero loss, which penalizes a model's value function prediction compared to the ground-truth value function, has been utilized in several prominent empirical works in the literature. However, theoretical investigation into its strengths and weaknesses is limited. In this paper, we analyze the family of value-aware model learning losses, which includes the popular MuZero loss. We show that these losses, as normally used, are uncalibrated surrogate losses, which means that they do not always recover the correct model and value function. Building on this insight, we propose corrections to solve this issue. Furthermore, we investigate the interplay between the loss calibration, latent model architectures, and auxiliary losses that are commonly employed when training MuZero-style agents. We show that while deterministic models can be sufficient to predict accurate values, learning calibrated stochastic models is still advantageous.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - sun254667/awesome-world-models: A curated, learning-friendly list of world model research resources: papers, datasets, benchmarks, simulation platforms, code, models, and tutorials, covering both classic foundational works and cutting-edge advances, catering to research initiation and industrial deployment. · GitHub (https://github.com/sun254667/awesome-world-models)\nciteturn41search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago;   * 🆕 τ₀-WM: A Unified Video-Action World Model for Robotic Manipulation ...   * 🆕 SC2-WM: A Self-Correcting World Model with Closed-Loop Feedback for Vision-and-Language Navigation in Continuous Environments\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDINO-WM: World Models on Pre-trained Visual Features enable Zero-shot Planning (https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.04983)\nciteturn41academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; We demonstrate that DINO-WM achieves zero-shot behavioral solutions at test time on six environments without expert demonstrations, reward modeling, or pre-learned inverse models, outperforming prior state-of-the-art work across diverse task families such as arbitrarily configured mazes, push manipulation with varied object shapes, and multi-particle scenarios.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNavForesee: A Unified Vision-Language World Model for Hierarchical Planning and Dual-Horizon Navigation Prediction | Papers | HyperAI (https://hyper.ai/en/papers/cvpr__2026__Liu_NavForesee_A_Unified_Vision-Language_World_Model_for_Hierarchical_Planning_and_CVPR_2026_paper)\nciteturn41search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Summary Paper Benchmarks ... Researchers from AMAP, Alibaba Group introduce NavForesee, a unified Vision-Language Model that concurrently performs hierarchical language planning and generative world-model prediction through an internal feedback loop of perception-planning/prediction-action, achieving competitive performan